 <p class="mod-desc">layer: alloc A set IS a map (`lib/map.smlib`) with a dummy value of 1: membership is map_contains, iteration is map_keys_bytes (R17: packed-byte segments, passed straight to the byte-key map functions with no repacking). ELEMENTS ARE BYTE STRINGS — the same choice as `map.smlib`&#x27;s keys, for the same reason: a caller passes a literal directly (&quot;apple&quot;), with an explicit length, and the whole strb_* surface (compare, pack, unpack) works on the elements unchanged. Do NOT pass char-code (qword-cell) arrays here; see the lib/strb.smlib header for the two representations. Consequences inherited from map: keys longer than 32 bytes are truncated to 32 on every operation, consistently. CAPACITY: a set&#x27;s capacity is fixed at set_init and stored in the buffer (m[0]). Set operations that write into `out` read out&#x27;s capacity from out[0], so the caller MUST pass a set_init&#x27;d buffer with room for the result: cap + bcap for union, cap for difference, min(cap, bcap) for intersection. If `out` is too small, map_put silently drops the overflowing elements (map&#x27;s full-table behaviour) and the returned size will be smaller than the true result — size the output generously and check the returned size. `out` for union / intersect / difference must be a FRESH, EMPTY set: the operations ADD into it and return map_size(out) afterwards. set_is_subset(a, ...) with an empty `a` is 1 (vacuous truth). The 6-argument cap still applies: union / intersect / difference keep the 5-argument shape from the round brief by reading out&#x27;s capacity from out[0] instead of taking it as a sixth parameter.</p>
